<article class="env-definition" id="030J">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/030J">Definition <span data-tag="030J">9.28.1</span>.</a> Algebraic field extensions. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
A field extension $K/k$ is called <em>algebraic</em> if every element of $K$ is algebraic over $k$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
An algebraic extension $k'/k$ is called <em>separable</em> if every $\alpha \in k'$ is separable over $k$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
An algebraic extension $k'/k$ is called <em>purely inseparable</em> if the characteristic of $k$ is $p > 0$ and for every element $\alpha \in k'$ there exists a power $q$ of $p$ such that $\alpha ^ q \in k$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
An algebraic extension $k'/k$ is called <em>normal</em> if for every $\alpha \in k'$ the minimal polynomial $P(T) \in k[T]$ of $\alpha $ over $k$ splits completely into linear factors over $k'$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
An algebraic extension $k'/k$ is called <em>Galois</em> if it is separable and normal. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>